---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { base } from "../../lib/site";
---
<Layout
  title="Evidence labels"
  description="How zhao-langxi marks Observed, Reported, Interpreted, and Open question in every essay."
  canonical="https://zhao-langxi.github.io/zhao-langxi/research/evidence-labels.html"
  current="research"
>
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <header>
      <p>Research and methods</p>
      <h1>Evidence labels</h1>
      <p>Use these in every essay and research note so a reader can see what kind of claim they are looking at.</p>
    </header>
    <article>
      <ul class="resource-list">
        <li>
          <h2>Observed</h2>
          <p>Something I personally witnessed or documented, with the date and surface named when it matters.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2>Reported</h2>
          <p>A claim supported by a linked source. Paraphrased, not treated as my own fieldwork.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2>Interpreted</h2>
          <p>My analysis. If the source disappeared, this part should still read as mine.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2>Open question</h2>
          <p>Not established yet. The useful ending of a note is often here, not in a slogan.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="essay-framing">
        Example: <strong>[Reported]</strong> a Smart LAB listing describes a digital-human customer-service tool.
        <strong>[Interpreted]</strong> that is evidence of what an institution published, not proof that users trust the face.
        <strong>[Open question]</strong> whether anyone comes back after the first answer.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href={`${base}research.html`}>Research hub</a> ·
        <a href={base}>Home</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
